<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni Pico della Mirandola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1463–1494</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni Pico della <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a> (1463–1494) was an Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> philosopher, a younger son of the lords of Mirandola. In 1486 he proposed to defend 900 theses in public debate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, introduced by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oration-on-the-dignity-of-man">Oration on the Dignity of Man</a>, but Pope Innocent VIII condemned several of them. He sought to reconcile Platonism, Aristotelianism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and the Kabbalah, and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, possibly poisoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-pico-della-mirandola.jpg" alt="Giovanni Pico della Mirandola" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cristofano dell'Altissimo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pico1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
